    "review": "The hero remained alive at full health when the planner stopped. Decision 2424 (log 6945) exposed a toxic gas trap blocking one corridor. Decisions 2453–2455 (logs 7032–7036) cleared a chilling plate but left a burning trap blocking the other corridor. Later public mapping revealed the exit beyond these barriers. The clearance controller supports chilling traps only; ordinary movement correctly rejects the remaining active plates. The bot instead spent its remaining wall-search allowance and stopped at decision 2549 (logs 7304–7305). The last accepted search advanced the clock without losing health. A fresh-policy, public-map classification probe found that hypothetically deactivating either blocker reconnects the exit, but this is not a verified execution plan. Offline action reconstruction validated decisions 0–623 and stopped at the first unhandled tactical recovery; later runtime memory was not reconstructed. No patch is proposed: extending cold-trap clearance would lack a demonstrated safe activation, hazard-expiry and retrieval transaction. In particular, fire can spread through the adjacent wooden door into mapped grass, while toxic gas is not confined to the cold controller's radius-one envelope. Complete selecting source was inspected; the remaining blocker is safety justification, not missing source. No red/green regression or candidate-suite result is claimed.",
    "learnings": "Distinguish an exhausted search allowance from an unresolved corridor prerequisite. Clearing one trap does not clear a multi-trap approach. Preserve active-trap exclusions, mandatory pickups and the stuck-planner outcome. A replacement must verify safe activation, retirement, hazard expiry and payload retrieval rather than infer safety from distance or mapped connectivity."
  },
